WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.859
One of the most popular ways to make a quick
buck in Hollywood is to rip off a hit.

00:00:04.859 --> 00:00:09.390
And that practice has a long, sketchy history
in the film industry around the world.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:15.429
After the massive success of 1977's Star Wars,
it seemed like everybody got caught in the

00:00:15.429 --> 00:00:20.400
space-craze, and the result was an entire
subgenre of mockbusters that obviously wanted

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.450
to be in a galaxy far, far away…but never
quite got there.

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:28.090
Here are a few of the strangest attempts to
rip-off Star Wars.

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:29.630
Message from Space

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:36.290
Everybody in the world made a Star Wars knockoff
in 1978, and Japan's legendary Toei Company

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:37.680
was no exception.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:43.070
Message from Space stole its look and characterizations
from Star Wars, and mixed it with manga and

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:45.750
anime — to generally bizarre results.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:50.329
The most striking thing about Message from
Space is that despite only having half the

00:00:50.329 --> 00:00:54.210
budget of A New Hope, the special effects
are surprisingly competent.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:55.960
Well, except for maybe this.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.120
Still, it pretty much owes its life to George
Lucas.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:12.130
Most obviously lifted from Star Wars is a
princess on the run, seeking help from wide-eyed

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:15.200
space jockeys, accompanied by a funny robot.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.670
On the other hand, Message from Space also
had its own dramatic Yoda death scene way

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.610
before Star Wars ever did.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:27.430
Aside from that, Message from Space feels
like the movie a person might make if they'd

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:30.850
heard vague descriptions of Star Wars, but
hadn't actually seen it.

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:36.150
A comedian in drag plays an evil empress,
and instead of the Force, there are space

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:41.380
walnuts that fly around choosing people to
do…something?

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.780
Still beats midichlorians, though.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:44.810
Starcrash

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:49.790
When most normal, well-adjusted humans watched
Star Wars, they didn't come away thinking:

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:53.270
"That movie was pretty great, but it needed
more weird swimsuits."

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:57.890
As it turned out, Italian director Luigi Cozzi
wasn't a normal person.

00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:03.630
He quickly whipped up the movie Starcrash
by 1979, a shameless Star Wars ripoff that

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:07.370
was basically an excuse to get nearly naked
women on camera.

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:13.690
Starcrash tells the story of a war between
a galactic Empire and the evil Count Zarth

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:17.480
Arn, who has a superweapon that the heroes
have to go blow up.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:22.190
Characters use lightsabers and fight evil
robotic minions, but once those similarities

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:27.420
are out of the way, Starcrash reaches a level
of incredible ridiculousness that George Lucas

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.450
could only touch in fever dreams.

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:33.860
"We've just survived and attack of the most
powerful weapon in the entire galaxy!"

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:37.540
"We have?"

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:43.220
Heroine Stella Star spends most of the movie
in a bikini, gets captured by a tribe of scantily

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:47.960
clad Amazons, is chased around by a giant
robot woman…

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:49.170
"Look!"

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:50.380
"Whoa!

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:52.040
Run for your lives!"

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:55.680
...gets frozen, and helps the Empire win their
giant space battle.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:59.040
Plus, as a bonus, a young and beautiful David
Hasselhoff.

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:07.660
Luigi Cozzi maintains that he came up with
the script before Star Wars came out, though,

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:08.790
so who knows?

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:12.060
Maybe George Lucas was the one making the
rip-off.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:15.260
Cozzi definitely beat him to when it came
to making one of the heroes the bad guy's

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:15.760
son.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:19.340
"Noooooo!!!"

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.090
Turkish Star Wars

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:28.650
Also known as…uh... words we can't hope
to pronounce, this movie is unique among other

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:32.959
ripoffs, because it literally lifts footage
straight from A New Hope and plops it into

00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:33.959
the movie.

00:03:33.959 --> 00:03:40.230
The Death Star, the Millennium Falcon, X-wings,
TIE Fighters, and the Mos Eisley Cantina — all

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:44.180
of these things can be found in Turkish Star
Wars.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:46.930
And that isn't the craziest thing about the
movie.

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:51.950
The film starts with two pilots, Murat and
Ali, crashing on a desert planet during a

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:52.950
space battle.

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.900
Past that, there isn't really any hope following
the plot.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:59.830
Ali accidentally summons a skeleton army by
whistling.

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:04.440
There's a planetary shield made of human brain
molecules that looks suspiciously like the

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:05.640
Death Star.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.070
Murat karate chops monsters in half.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:15.040
Somewhere along the way, a story happens.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.519
When the movie came out, 20th Century Fox
was evidently so stumped by it that they didn't

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:20.930
even try to sue.

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:24.370
Even today, Disney-owned Lucasfilm still hasn't
taken action.

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:26.910
It's so bad they don't even view it as threat.

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:31.320
But Star Wars probably would have been even
cooler if Luke ripped the head off of a Wampa.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.620
The Shape of Things to Come

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:43.520
In 1979, Canadian director George McCowen
set out to make his own Star Wars knockoff,

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:47.900
buying the rights to H.G. Wells' 1933 novel
The Shape of Things to Come.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:52.871
Since Canada isn't really known as a science
fiction hotspot, it's easy to guess how things

00:04:52.871 --> 00:04:53.871
turned out.

00:04:53.871 --> 00:04:59.210
The film was already off to a rough start
because H.G. Wells' book is actually a speculative

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:05.990
novel about the future of the world from 1933
to 2106, and it features a distinct lack of

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:07.090
robots.

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:09.940
Literally none of the things in the novel
happen in the movie.

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:13.770
McCowen just shamelessly bought the rights
just so he could use a title people might

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:15.190
know for his movie.

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:18.960
And like most movies of the era, The Shape
of Things to Come had a pretty low-budget

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:19.960
look.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:25.400
Plotwise, it's pretty much Star Wars, just
set on Earth's moon after a "Robot War."

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:30.180
There's a bad guy who has a massive fortress,
and a resistance movement trying to stop him.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:34.740
McCowen thought if he made the bad guy use
robot drones as his minions, nobody would

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.699
know it was a shameless ripoff.

00:05:36.699 --> 00:05:37.790
He was wrong.

00:05:37.790 --> 00:05:40.110
Yor: The Hunter from the Future

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:44.680
At first, Yor doesn't look much a Star Wars
ripoff.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:49.740
It starts as a barbarian sword-and-loincloth
feature set to the sweet sounds of '80s hair

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:50.740
metal.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:55.580
We've got a guy hunting dinosaurs, meeting
magical people and seducing willing wenches.

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:57.830
Who could possibly hate that?

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:02.370
Then, suddenly, it's like the movie gets bored
and goes full-on science fiction.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:07.020
The second half of the movie is about an evil
organization led by a guy dressed in black

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:11.270
robes that wouldn't look out of place in Knights
of the Old Republic, evil minions who wear

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:15.169
knockoff Darth Vader masks, and laser battles.

00:06:15.169 --> 00:06:20.040
Spoiler alert: turns out that Yor has actually
been living on a post-apocalyptic, sci-fi

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:21.070
Earth.

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:22.360
With dinosaurs.

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:24.680
Which is pretty much every eight-year-old's
dream.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:28.630
This wasn't even just a case of the screenwriter
mashing stuff together.

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:33.040
It's actually based off an Argentinian comic
book of the same name, which was evidently

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:35.570
popular enough to bring to the big screen.

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:39.550
It's really not that bad an idea for a movie,
but Yor deserves better.

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:41.370
Starchaser: The Legend of Orin

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:47.620
It's the story of a young miner who discovers
a magic sword and embarks on an epic quest

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:53.119
to destroy an evil space empire with the help
of robot sidekicks and a rogue criminal.

00:06:53.119 --> 00:06:55.479
And it's not Star Wars.

00:06:55.479 --> 00:07:00.550
Despite being an animated film, Starchaser:
The Legend of Orin is waaay less kid-friendly

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:01.639
than Star Wars.

00:07:01.639 --> 00:07:05.570
The characters swear a lot more and the violence
is even worse.

00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:07.990
And then there's the, erm, fembot.

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:13.520
The heroes pick up an oddly curvy, totally-not-C3PO
lady-droid during their adventures.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:15.699
Like C-3PO, she's pretty annoying.

00:07:15.699 --> 00:07:17.450
So how do our heroes deal with it?

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:21.690
They stick probes in her robot butt to reprogram
her.

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:24.510
Into a full-on sexbot.

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:29.190
Despite offering a terrifying view into the
filmmaker's fantasies, Starchaser was pretty

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:30.370
groundbreaking.

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:35.010
It was one of the first 3D animated movies,
and one of the first to combine computer graphics

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:37.010
with traditional hand-drawn animation.

00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:41.500
Thankfully, those technologies were also used
to make movies that weren't creepy Star Wars

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:42.570
ripoffs.

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:43.639
Space Mutiny

00:07:43.639 --> 00:07:49.500
In 1978, Glen Larson managed to get Battlestar
Galactica off the ground, after years of shopping

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:52.460
it around, because of the success of Star
Wars.

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:57.050
Some might call it a total ripoff, but it
found plenty of success in its own right.

00:07:57.050 --> 00:08:01.250
Enough success that a South African production
company wanted to cash in on it ten years

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:02.250
later.

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:04.539
The result was Space Mutiny.

00:08:04.539 --> 00:08:08.789
Without the money for special effects, the
production just decided to go the easy route

00:08:08.789 --> 00:08:13.880
and steal effects shots from Galactica, use
whatever office equipment they had around,

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:19.380
and film the thing in brick buildings, which
were supposed to represent areas of a spaceship.

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:25.360
The story revolves around a mutiny on a colony
ship, but the plot is skeletal at best.

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:30.210
Characters die, only to re-appear in the next
scene, plot elements appear out of order,

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:33.089
and it feels like key pieces of dialogue were
cut.

00:08:33.089 --> 00:08:35.039
That doesn't mean the movie is without charm.

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:40.289
It has an awesome go-cart chase through the
"engine room" of the ship, a cool hula-hoop

00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:45.320
dance club, and B-movie icon Cameron Mitchell
hanging around in a bathrobe.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:50.360
The strangest element of the whole fiasco
is that by 1988, ripoffs just weren't profitable

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:54.910
anymore, and the movie has too much stolen
footage to stand on its own.

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:58.449
How they never got sued remains a space-mystery.

00:08:58.449 --> 00:08:59.449
Battle Beyond the Stars

00:08:59.449 --> 00:09:05.149
Schlock auteur Roger Corman threw his hat
in the Star Wars ripoff ring with his 1980

00:09:05.149 --> 00:09:07.759
film Battle Beyond the Stars.

00:09:07.759 --> 00:09:11.440
For those who aren't familiar with Corman's
body of work, the dude was well known for

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:15.970
making fast, cheap movies to capitalize on
whatever craze was going on at the time.

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:20.290
He knew how to stretch a budget and made the
most semi-competent movies he could, and Battle

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:23.990
Beyond the Stars was his most expensive movie
to date, coming in at $2 million.

00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:27.379
As a result, the film ended up being surprisingly
competent.

00:09:27.379 --> 00:09:32.259
It looked and felt just like Star Wars, but
was more of a loose remake of Seven Samurai

00:09:32.259 --> 00:09:33.269
in space.

00:09:33.269 --> 00:09:36.279
Most importantly, the plot actually kinda
makes sense.

00:09:36.279 --> 00:09:39.829
Shockingly, it also had some future big names
attached.

00:09:39.829 --> 00:09:42.970
James Cameron was in charge of special effects
and art direction.

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:47.269
James Horner wrote the score and liked it
so much that he basically ripped himself off

00:09:47.269 --> 00:09:49.260
and reused it for The Wrath of Khan.

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:52.180
"Khannnn!!!!"

00:09:52.199 --> 00:09:56.870
All of that made Battle Beyond the Stars the
most skillfully made ripoff from the era.

00:09:56.870 --> 00:10:01.269
Even better, Corman, being the cheapskate
he was, didn't break down the sets after filming

00:10:01.269 --> 00:10:05.880
and just re-used them for yet another Star
Wars ripoff, Space Raiders.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:08.899
That guy knew how to get the biggest bang
for his buck.

00:10:08.899 --> 00:10:09.899
The Black Hole

00:10:09.899 --> 00:10:14.889
Storywise, Disney's legendarily expensive
The Black Hole actually has more in common

00:10:14.889 --> 00:10:16.810
with Star Trek than Star Wars.

00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:22.079
But with bumbling robot sidekicks, spaceships
that look like Mos Eisley spaceport rejects,

00:10:22.079 --> 00:10:25.369
and costumes that look like they're fresh
out of the Death Star's supply closet, the

00:10:25.369 --> 00:10:27.369
Star Wars connection is undeniable.

00:10:27.369 --> 00:10:31.149
What makes this movie so bizarre is that it
gets incredibly dark.

00:10:31.149 --> 00:10:35.709
Robot villain Maximilian is a frightening
cross between Darth Vader and a Cylon.

00:10:35.709 --> 00:10:39.670
Biblical allusions and gruesome murder lurk
around every plot point.

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:41.559
Humans get melded with robots.

00:10:41.559 --> 00:10:45.569
And of course, we can't forget about the final
scene, in which the characters are sucked

00:10:45.569 --> 00:10:50.499
into the black hole only to find the most
convincing interpretation of hell ever set

00:10:50.499 --> 00:10:51.499
on film.

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:52.760
Sleep well, Mouseketeers!

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:01.839
There's arguably a good movie in the middle
of The Black Hole, but Disney couldn't decide

00:11:01.839 --> 00:11:07.129
whether to make it child-appropriate, or something
more heady like 2001: A Space Odyssey.

00:11:07.129 --> 00:11:10.649
Disney would have to wait a few decades to
make actual Star Wars films.

00:11:10.649 --> 00:11:14.889
Amazingly, the studio has also started working
on remaking The Black Hole.

00:11:14.889 --> 00:11:18.269
No word yet on whether Satan will make an
appearance this time.

00:11:18.269 --> 00:11:19.319
Thanks for watching!

00:11:19.319 --> 00:11:21.819
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:11:21.819 --> 00:11:23.999
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

